\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}
This section presents the proposed hybrid deep learning framework for IoT DDoS detection. The overall architecture of the system is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_pipeline}, which depicts the complete pipeline from data acquisition to explainability.

\begin{figure*}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{figures/Model_Pipeline_Architecture.eps}
\caption{Proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM-Attention framework for IoT DDoS detection}
\label{fig:proposed_pipeline}
\end{figure*}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_pipeline}, the proposed framework consists of multiple sequential stages: data acquisition, preprocessing and feature harmonisation, temporal sequence construction, parallel spatial-temporal feature extraction (via CNN and BiLSTM branches), attention-based feature refinement, classification, and multilevel explainability. Each component is mathematically formulated and integrated to address the challenges of heterogeneous high-velocity IoT network flows.

\subsection{Problem Definition}

Let $\mathcal{D}^{(k)} = \{(x_i^{(k)}, y_i^{(k)})\}_{i=1}^{N_k}$ denote the $k$-th IoT dataset under analysis, where $N_k$ represents the number of flow samples. Here, $x_i^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_k}$ is the raw feature vector representing the network packet headers and flow statistics in a $d_k$-dimensional feature space, and $y_i^{(k)}$ is the target label, respectively. For binary threat detection, the labels belong to the space $y_i^{(k)} \in \mathcal{Y}_{bin} = \{0, 1\}$, where $0$ represents benign traffic and $1$ represents a DDoS attack. For multiclass intrusion categorisation, the labels belong to the space $y_i^{(k)} \in \mathcal{Y}_{multi} = \{0, 1, \dots, C-1\}$, where $C$ is the number of distinct traffic subclasses (benign traffic plus specific DDoS attack vectors such as HTTP flood, UDP flood, and TCP SYN flood, etc.).

Given $K$ heterogeneous IoT datasets with different dimensionality ($d_1 \neq d_2 \neq \dots \neq d_K$) and class schemas, our goal is to design a unified mapping operator and learn a robust, generalisable classifier $f_\theta: \mathbb{R}^d \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}$ parameterised by weights $\theta$ that minimises the detection error across both intra-dataset and cross-dataset evaluation setups.

\subsection{Feature Harmonization Across Datasets}

Owing to the heterogeneous feature sets produced by different network collectors, we define a unified target feature space $\mathcal{F}_u \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ of dimension $d$. We define a projection operator $\phi(\cdot)$ that maps the heterogeneous input vector $x_i^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_k}$ to the unified feature space:

\begin{equation}
x_i' = \phi(x_i^{(k)}) \in \mathbb{R}^{d}
\end{equation}

The mapping operator $\phi(\cdot)$ performs:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Schema Alignment:} Mapping matching feature keys (e.g., flow duration, source port, packet length) and zero-padding missing dimensions that are not present in dataset $k$ but are required in the unified space.
    \item \textbf{Imputation:} Replacing missing, infinite, or corrupted values with localized statistical estimators (nearest-neighbor mean).
    \item \textbf{Encoding:} Projecting categorical fields (e.g., protocol types) using target encoding and sparse one-hot mapping.
\end{enumerate}

To ensure numerical stability during gradient backpropagation and prevent features with larger scales from dominating the loss, Z-score normalisation was applied to each feature dimension:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}_i = \frac{x_i' - \mu^{(k)}}{\sigma^{(k)}}
\end{equation}

where $\mu^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $\sigma^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ are the mean and standard deviation vectors computed over the training split of dataset $k$, respectively.

\subsection{Temporal Sequence Construction}

To capture the sequential nature of DDoS attacks, which often manifest as volumetric patterns over time rather than isolated packet anomalies, static normalised feature vectors are transformed into sliding temporal sequences.

Let $\mathbf{x}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be the normalised feature vector at flow index $i$. A temporal sequence matrix $\mathbf{X}_i$ is constructed using a lookback window of size $T$:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_i = [\mathbf{x}_{i-T+1}, \mathbf{x}_{i-T+2}, \dots, \mathbf{x}_i]^T \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times d}
\end{equation}

This sequence matrix preserves the temporal flow dynamics, allowing the model to capture the short-term temporal dependencies and flow-level correlations.

\subsection{Hybrid CNN--BiLSTM Architecture}

The proposed hybrid model processes the temporal sequence matrix $\mathbf{X}_i$ using two parallel neural branches: a 1D convolutional neural network (CNN) branch to extract spatial correlations and localised feature interactions, and a bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM) branch to model long-range temporal dependencies. The parameter complexity of the proposed model varies depending on the input feature dimensionality and classification task (binary vs. multiclass). Table~\ref{tab:modelsize} summarises the input dimensions, number of classes, and parameter count for the proposed model across all evaluated datasets.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Proposed Hybrid CNN--BiLSTM Fusion Model Parameter Counts Across Datasets}
\label{tab:modelsize}
\begin{tabular}{lcccr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Input Features} & \textbf{Task Type} & \textbf{Classes} & \textbf{Parameters} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{Apose-IoT-23} & \multirow{2}{*}{18} & Binary & 2 & 153,602 \\
 & & Multiclass & 5 & 153,797 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{BoT-IoT} & \multirow{2}{*}{29} & Binary & 2 & 156,418 \\
 & & Multiclass & 8 & 156,808 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{CIC-IoMT-2024} & \multirow{2}{*}{46} & Binary & 2 & 160,770 \\
 & & Multiclass & 19 & 161,875 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{CIC-IoT-2025} & \multirow{2}{*}{94} & Binary & 2 & 173,058 \\
 & & Multiclass & 8 & 173,448 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The computational pipeline of each parallel branch is described as follows.

\subsubsection{CNN Branch (Spatial Feature Learning)}

The spatial feature extraction branch uses a 1D convolutional layer to capture local relations across neighbouring features in the sequence. Given the input matrix $\mathbf{X}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times d}$, a set of $M$ filters of kernel size $K_{conv}$ are convolved along the temporal dimension. The activation value $h_{t, m}$ of the $m$-th filter at step $t$ is computed as:

\begin{equation}
h_{t, m} = \sigma \left( \sum_{k=1}^{K_{conv}} \sum_{j=1}^{d} W_{m, k, j} X_{t+k-1, j} + b_m \right)
\end{equation}

where $W_m \in \mathbb{R}^{K_{conv} \times d}$ is the weight tensor of filter $m$, $b_m$ is the bias term, and $\sigma(\cdot)$ represents the rectified linear unit (ReLU) activation function: $\sigma(z) = \max(0, z)$.

To downsample the spatial representation and retain only the most prominent feature excitations, a 1D Max-Pooling layer with a pool size of $K_{pool}$ is applied as follows:

\begin{equation}
p_{i, m} = \max_{j \in [1, K_{pool}]} h_{(i-1)K_{pool} + j, m}
\end{equation}

The pooled feature maps are then flattened to yield the spatial representation vector $f_{cnn}$:

\begin{equation}
f_{cnn} = \text{Flatten}(\mathbf{P}) \in \mathbb{R}^{D_{cnn}}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{BiLSTM Branch (Temporal Modeling)}

While the CNN branch extracts spatial feature interactions, the BiLSTM branch models temporal dependencies in both the forward and backward directions. This bidirectional processing is essential for network flow security because packet interactions depend on both the preceding and succeeding network states.

For the input sequence $\mathbf{X}_i = [\mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_T]^T$, a standard forward LSTM cell processes the sequence from $t = 1$ to $T$, updating its hidden state $\overrightarrow{h}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{lstm}}$ and cell state $c_t \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{lstm}}$ through the following gating mechanisms:

\begin{align}
i_t &= \sigma_{sig} \left( W_{xi} \mathbf{x}_t + W_{hi} \overrightarrow{h}_{t-1} + b_i \right) \\
f_t &= \sigma_{sig} \left( W_{xf} \mathbf{x}_t + W_{hf} \overrightarrow{h}_{t-1} + b_f \right) \\
o_t &= \sigma_{sig} \left( W_{xo} \mathbf{x}_t + W_{ho} \overrightarrow{h}_{t-1} + b_o \right) \\
\tilde{c}_t &= \tanh \left( W_{xc} \mathbf{x}_t + W_{hc} \overrightarrow{h}_{t-1} + b_c \right) \\
c_t &= f_t \odot c_{t-1} + i_t \odot \tilde{c}_t \\
\overrightarrow{h}_t &= o_t \odot \tanh(c_t)
\end{align}

where $i_t$, $f_t$, and $o_t$ represent the input, forget, and output gates, respectively; $\tilde{c}_t$ is the candidate cell state; $\sigma_{sig}(z) = (1 + e^{-z})^{-1}$ is the sigmoid activation; and $\odot$ denotes element-wise vector multiplication.

Concurrently, a backward LSTM cell processes the sequence in reverse order (from $t = T$ to $1$) to capture the historical flow context.

\begin{equation}
\overleftarrow{h}_t = \text{LSTM}_{back}(\mathbf{x}_t, \overleftarrow{h}_{t+1})
\end{equation}

The final temporal representation $f_{lstm}$ is constructed by concatenating the final hidden states of both directional sweeps as follows:

\begin{equation}
f_{lstm} = [\overrightarrow{h}_T \oplus \overleftarrow{h}_1] \in \mathbb{R}^{2 d_{lstm}}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Feature Fusion}

The spatial feature vector $f_{cnn}$ and temporal feature vector $f_{lstm}$ are concatenated to create the joint representation vector $f$:

\begin{equation}
f = [f_{cnn} \oplus f_{lstm}] \in \mathbb{R}^{D_{fused}}
\end{equation}

where $D_{fused} = D_{cnn} + 2 d_{lstm}$.

\subsection{Attention-Based Feature Refinement}

To prevent the model from being overloaded by noisy or redundant spatial-temporal features, a feature-level self-attention mechanism is applied. This layer assigns dynamic weights to the components of the fused feature vector $f$, emphasising critical feature correlations.

First, the fused vector is passed through a dense layer to project it into an attention energy space.

\begin{equation}
e = \tanh(W_a f + b_a)
\end{equation}

where $W_a \in \mathbb{R}^{D_{fused} \times D_{fused}}$ is the attention weight matrix and $b_a \in \mathbb{R}^{D_{fused}}$ is the attention bias vector.

The attention weights $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}^{D_{fused}}$ are computed using a softmax activation function:

\begin{equation}
\alpha_i = \frac{\exp(e_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^{D_{fused}} \exp(e_j)}
\end{equation}

The refined representation $f'$ is obtained by applying the attention weights element-wise as follows:

\begin{equation}
f' = \alpha \odot f
\end{equation}

This dynamic weighting suppresses noisy, non-contributing dimensions and highlights relevant spatial-temporal correlations.

\subsection{Classification Layer}

The refined representation $f'$ is mapped to the target output space using a fully connected layer as follows:

\begin{equation}
z = W_o f' + b_o
\end{equation}

where $W_o \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times D_{fused}}$ is the projection matrix, $b_o \in \mathbb{R}^{C}$ is the bias, and $C$ is the number of target classes.

The final predicted probability distribution $\hat{y}$ is obtained using the softmax function as follows:

\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_c = \frac{\exp(z_c)}{\sum_{j=1}^{C} \exp(z_j)}, \quad c \in [1, C]
\end{equation}

\subsection{Training Objective}

The network $\theta = \{W_c, b_c, W_{x\cdot}, W_{h\cdot}, b_{\cdot}, W_a, b_a, W_o, b_o\}$ are optimised end-to-end by minimising the categorical cross-entropy loss function $\mathcal{L}$ combined with an $L_2$ regularisation penalty to prevent overfitting:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}(\theta) = - \frac{1}{B} \sum_{i=1}^{B} \sum_{c=1}^{C} y_{i, c} \log(\hat{y}_{i, c}) + \lambda \|\theta\|_2^2
\end{equation}

where $B$ is the batch size, $y_{i, c}$ is the ground-truth label (one-hot encoded), and $\lambda$ is the weight decay regularisation coefficient. The optimisation was performed using the AdamW optimiser.

\subsection{Algorithms}

The proposed framework is executed in two primary algorithmic procedures. Algorithm~\ref{alg:preprocessing} defines the dataset harmonisation and temporal sequence construction pipelines. Algorithm~\ref{alg:training} describes the end-to-end training, optimisation, and explainability verification processes.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Dataset Harmonization and Temporal Sequence Construction Pipeline}
\label{alg:preprocessing}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Raw heterogeneous IoT network datasets $\{\mathcal{D}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}$, target feature space $\mathcal{F}_u$, sequence window length $T$
\ENSURE Harmonized temporal sequence datasets $\{\mathcal{T}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\FOR{each dataset $k \in [1, K]$}
    \STATE Align features to match the unified schema: $\mathbf{x}_i' = \phi(\mathbf{x}_i^{(k)})$
    \STATE Impute missing or infinite values using nearest-neighbor statistical imputation
    \STATE Encode categorical variables using target and sparse one-hot mapping
    \STATE Compute feature-wise mean vector $\mu^{(k)}$ and standard deviation vector $\sigma^{(k)}$ over the training split
    \STATE Normalize aligned vectors: $\mathbf{x}_i^{norm} = (\mathbf{x}_i' - \mu^{(k)}) / \sigma^{(k)}$
    \STATE Initialize sequence set $\mathcal{T}^{(k)} \leftarrow \emptyset$
    \FOR{each instance index $i$ from $T$ to $N_k$}
        \STATE Construct sequence matrix: $\mathbf{X}_i = [\mathbf{x}_{i-T+1}^{norm}, \mathbf{x}_{i-T+2}^{norm}, \dots, \mathbf{x}_i^{norm}]^T \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times d}$
        \STATE Append sequence-label pair $(\mathbf{X}_i, y_i^{(k)})$ to $\mathcal{T}^{(k)}$
    \ENDFOR
\ENDFOR
\RETURN $\{\mathcal{T}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{End-to-End Training and Explainable Verification of the Hybrid Framework}
\label{alg:training}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Harmonized sequence datasets $\{\mathcal{T}^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}$, epoch count $E$, batch size $B$, learning rate $\eta$, regularization coefficient $\lambda$
\ENSURE Optimized network parameters $\theta$, joint explainability profile $\mathcal{I}(x)$
\STATE Initialize model parameters $\theta = \{W_c, b_c, W_{x\cdot}, W_{h\cdot}, b_{\cdot}, W_a, b_a, W_o, b_o\}$ randomly
\FOR{each epoch $e \in [1, E]$}
    \FOR{each batch $\mathcal{B} \subset \mathcal{T}^{(k)}$ of size $B$}
        \STATE \textbf{Forward Pass:}
        \FOR{each sequence $\mathbf{X}_i \in \mathcal{B}$}
            \STATE Extract spatial features using 1D CNN: $f_{cnn, i} = \text{Flatten}(\text{MaxPool}(\sigma(W_c * \mathbf{X}_i + b_c)))$
            \STATE Extract temporal features using forward/backward LSTM cells: $f_{lstm, i} = [\overrightarrow{h}_T \oplus \overleftarrow{h}_1]_i$
            \STATE Concatenate branch outputs: $f_i = [f_{cnn, i} \oplus f_{lstm, i}]$
            \STATE Compute attention energy: $e_i = \tanh(W_a f_i + b_a)$
            \STATE Normalize attention weights: $\alpha_i = \text{softmax}(e_i)$
            \STATE Compute refined feature vector: $f'_i = \alpha_i \odot f_i$
            \STATE Compute output class logits: $z_i = W_o f'_i + b_o$
            \STATE Predict class probabilities: $\hat{y}_i = \text{softmax}(z_i)$
        \ENDFOR
        \STATE \textbf{Loss Computation:}
        \STATE Compute CCE loss with L2 regularization: $\mathcal{L}(\theta) = - \frac{1}{B} \sum_{i=1}^B \sum_{c=1}^C y_{i,c} \log(\hat{y}_{i,c}) + \lambda \|\theta\|_2^2$
        \STATE \textbf{Backward Pass and Optimization:}
        \STATE Compute gradients $\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}(\theta)$ using backpropagation
        \STATE Update network parameters $\theta$ using AdamW: $\theta \leftarrow \text{AdamW}(\theta, \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}(\theta), \eta)$
    \ENDFOR
\ENDFOR
\STATE \textbf{Explainability Evaluation:}
\STATE Compute global SHAP values $\phi^{SHAP}$ on $f_\theta$ using sampling approximations
\STATE Fit local LIME explainer for selected instances $x$ to obtain local surrogate weights $\phi^{LIME}$
\STATE Perform ANOVA F-statistic test on features to obtain statistical significance values $F^{ANOVA}$
\STATE Construct joint explanation profile: $\mathcal{I}(x) = \{\phi^{SHAP}, \phi^{LIME}, F^{ANOVA}\}$
\RETURN Optimized parameters $\theta$, Joint Explanations $\mathcal{I}(x)$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Computational Complexity}

The computational complexity of the proposed framework is analyzed component-by-component below, where $N$ represents the number of training samples, $T$ is the sequence lookback length, and $d$ is the feature dimension:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CNN Branch:} The complexity of convolving $M$ filters of size $K_{conv}$ over sequences is $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot T \cdot d \cdot M \cdot K_{conv})$. Since the kernel size and filters are fixed hyperparameters, this simplifies to $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot T \cdot d)$.
    \item \textbf{BiLSTM Branch:} The computational complexity of updating the input, forget, and output gates for both forward and backward cells is $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot T \cdot (d \cdot d_{lstm} + d_{lstm}^2))$. Assuming the hidden dimension $d_{lstm}$ is proportional to the feature dimension $d$, this yields $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot T \cdot d^2)$.
    \item \textbf{Attention and Classification Layers:} The matrix products and softmax computations require $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot D_{fused}^2)$ and $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot C \cdot D_{fused})$ operations, respectively. Since $D_{fused}$ scales linearly with $d$, this simplifies to $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot d^2)$.
\end{itemize}

By combining these components, the overall training complexity per epoch is dominated by the temporal recurrences of the BiLSTM cells:

\begin{equation}
\text{Complexity}_{overall} = \mathcal{O}(N \cdot T \cdot d^2)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Explainable Artificial Intelligence Framework}

An XAI module was incorporated to enhance the interpretability, transparency, and trustworthiness of the proposed hybrid model. In complex security-critical applications such as IoT intrusion detection, black-box deep learning architectures are often rejected by network administrators due to their lack of auditability. To address this issue, we propose a joint explainability framework that integrates SHAP, LIME, and ANOVA-based analyses. 

These three methods represent complementary paradigms of explainability, designed to provide a multi-level validation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Global Feature Attribution (SHAP):} Built on cooperative game theory, SHAP provides mathematically consistent and axiomatic global importance attributions. It evaluates feature significance as average marginal contributions across all possible coalitions, ensuring that the general behavior of the hybrid model is globally sound and consistent.
    \item \textbf{Local Flow-Level Fidelity (LIME):} While SHAP outlines global patterns, it is computationally intensive and provides averaged indicators that might mask high-fidelity localized anomalies. LIME solves this gap by approximating the decision boundary locally around a single network flow instance using a sparse linear surrogate model. This is critical for security analysts who require instant, flow-specific reasoning (e.g. why a particular session was flagged) during real-time threat triage.
    \item \textbf{Empirical Statistical Filtering (ANOVA):} While SHAP and LIME depend on the trained parameters of the deep learning model, ANOVA provides an independent, statistical baseline of feature variance between normal and malicious groups. By evaluating the ratio of between-group to within-group variance, ANOVA validates whether the feature signals captured by the deep neural network represent actual statistically significant physical variations in the data distribution, rather than artifacts of model overfitting.
\end{itemize}
This three-tiered validation ensures that the explanation of the model's decisions is globally consistent, locally high-fidelity, and statistically validated.

\subsubsection{SHAP: Global Feature Importance}

SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) are used to quantify the global contributions of features to model predictions based on cooperative game theory.

Given a trained model $f_\theta$ and an input feature vector $x$, the prediction is expressed as a linear additive function of the feature attributions:

\begin{equation}
f_\theta(x) = \phi_0 + \sum_{i=1}^{d} \phi_i
\end{equation}

where $\phi_0$ is the base value (expected model output over the training set), and $\phi_i$ represents the Shapley contribution value of feature $i$.

The Shapley value $\phi_i$ is computed as the weighted average marginal contribution of feature $i$ across all possible feature subsets as follows:

\begin{equation}
\phi_i = \sum_{S \subseteq F \setminus \{i\}} \frac{|S|!(|F|-|S|-1)!}{|F|!} \left[ f_\theta(S \cup \{i\}) - f_\theta(S) \right]
\end{equation}

where $F$ is the complete set of input features, and $S$ is a subset of features excluding feature $i$. This formulation guarantees three fundamental properties: local accuracy, missingness, and consistency.

\subsubsection{LIME: Local Interpretability}

Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) explain individual predictions by locally approximating the decision boundary of a complex model using an interpretable surrogate model.

For a target instance $x$, LIME minimises a loss function representing the discrepancy between the complex model $f$ and the surrogate model $g \in G$ (e.g. a sparse linear model) over a perturbed neighbourhood $Z$:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}(f, g, \pi_x) = \sum_{z \in Z} \pi_x(z) \left( f(z) - g(z) \right)^2
\end{equation}

where $\pi_x(z)$ is the proximity measure between the target instance $x$ and perturbed instance $z$, defined using an exponential kernel:

\begin{equation}
\pi_x(z) = \exp \left( -\frac{D(x,z)^2}{\sigma_{lime}^2} \right)
\end{equation}

where $D(x,z)$ represents a distance metric (such as Euclidean distance) and $\sigma_{lime}$ denotes the kernel width.

The optimal local explanation $g^*$ is obtained by solving

\begin{equation}
g^* = \arg\min_{g \in G} \mathcal{L}(f, g, \pi_x) + \Omega(g)
\end{equation}

where $\Omega(g)$ is a complexity penalty (e.g. limiting the number of nonzero coefficients in a linear model).

\subsubsection{ANOVA: Statistical Feature Significance}

To statistically validate the discriminative power of the features across target traffic classes (benign and attack subclasses), we used a one-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) F-statistic significance test.

The F-statistic evaluates whether the variance between different traffic groups is significantly larger than the variance within the groups.

\begin{equation}
F = \frac{MS_{between}}{MS_{within}} = \frac{SS_B / (K - 1)}{SS_W / (N - K)}
\end{equation}

where $SS_B$ is the sum of squares between groups (between-class variance).

\begin{equation}
SS_B = \sum_{k=1}^{K} n_k (\bar{x}_k - \bar{x})^2
\end{equation}

and $SS_W$ is the sum of squares within groups (within-class variance).

\begin{equation}
SS_W = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \sum_{i=1}^{n_k} (x_{i, k} - \bar{x}_k)^2
\end{equation}

where $K$ represents the number of classes, $n_k$ is the sample count in class $k$, $\bar{x}_k$ denotes the class-specific mean of the feature, and $\bar{x}$ represents the global feature mean. Features with higher F-values and extremely small p-values ($p < 0.001$) are statistically relevant.

\subsubsection{Integration with Hybrid Model}

The joint explainability framework combines these three methods to construct a unified explanation profile $\mathcal{I}(x)$ for any network flow $x$:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}(x) = \{\phi_i^{SHAP}, \phi_i^{LIME}, F_i^{ANOVA}\}
\end{equation}

This multi-level structure guarantees three distinct layers of explanation:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Layer 1: Empirical Soundness (ANOVA):} Ensures that the feature selection is statistically grounded ($F_i^{ANOVA} \gg 1$) and corresponds to physical deviations in traffic distribution.
    \item \textbf{Layer 2: Global Consistency (SHAP):} Mathematically allocates contribution scores ($\phi_i^{SHAP}$) across the feature space, ensuring the overall neural network logic aligns with domain knowledge.
    \item \textbf{Layer 3: Local Actionability (LIME):} Offers flow-specific feature importance ($\phi_i^{LIME}$) that maps directly to the active traffic characteristics of a detected attack.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Computational Considerations}

The computational complexity of the explainability module is defined as:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{SHAP complexity:} $\mathcal{O}(2^d)$ (approximated using KernelSHAP or TreeSHAP to ensure scalability).
    \item \textbf{LIME complexity:} $\mathcal{O}(M \cdot d)$, where $M$ is the number of perturbations.
    \item \textbf{ANOVA complexity:} $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot d)$ which scales linearly with the dataset size.
\end{itemize}

\section{Datasets and Preprocessing}

This section describes the datasets utilised in this study and the preprocessing steps performed to ensure the compatibility, consistency, and robustness of heterogeneous IoT network traffic data sources.

\subsection{Datasets Description}

To evaluate the proposed framework, four publicly available IoT intrusion detection datasets were utilised:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Apose-IoT-23}: A recent dataset containing realistic IoT network traffic with labeled attack and benign flows.
    \item \textbf{BoT-IoT}: A widely used benchmark dataset comprising various botnet-based attacks including DDoS, DoS, and reconnaissance.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoMT-2024}: A dataset focused on Internet of Medical Things (IoMT), capturing healthcare-related IoT traffic with multiple attack scenarios.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoT-2025}: A large-scale and modern IoT dataset incorporating diverse attack patterns and real-world traffic distributions.
\end{itemize}

Each dataset differs in feature dimensionality, traffic representation, and attack taxonomy, which makes cross-dataset learning challenging.

Let the collection of datasets be denoted as

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D} = \{\mathcal{D}^{(1)}, \mathcal{D}^{(2)}, \dots, \mathcal{D}^{(K)}\}
\end{equation}

where $K = 4$ in this study.



\subsection{Label Unification}

Different datasets use different labelling schemes. Therefore, all labels were mapped into a binary classification framework as follows:

\begin{equation}
y =
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if attack (DDoS/DoS)} \\
0, & \text{if benign}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

This transformation ensured consistency across the datasets used in this study.

\subsection{Feature Harmonization}

Owing to the heterogeneity of feature spaces, a unified feature representation is required.

Let $\mathcal{F}^{(k)}$ denote feature set of dataset $k$. The common feature space is defined as:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_u = \bigcap_{k=1}^{K} \mathcal{F}^{(k)}
\end{equation}

Each sample is projected into $\mathcal{F}_u$:

\begin{equation}
x_i' = \phi(x_i^{(k)})
\end{equation}

where $\phi(\cdot)$ performs:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Feature alignment
    \item Column matching
    \item Removal of incompatible attributes
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Missing Value Handling}

Missing values may arise because of feature mismatches or incomplete records.

Let $x_j$ denote a feature. Missing values are handled as:

\begin{equation}
x_j =
\begin{cases}
\text{mean}(x_j), & \text{if numerical} \\
\text{mode}(x_j), & \text{if categorical}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}



\subsection{Categorical Encoding}

Categorical attributes were transformed using one-hot encoding.

\begin{equation}
x_{cat} \rightarrow \text{OneHot}(x_{cat})
\end{equation}



\subsection{Feature Normalization}

To ensure stable training, all features were normalised using Z-score normalisation.

\begin{equation}
x^{norm} = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}
\end{equation}

where $\mu$ and $\sigma$ denote the mean and standard deviation, respectively.



\subsection{Temporal Sequence Construction}

Because network traffic exhibits temporal behaviour, feature vectors are transformed into sequences using a sliding window approach.

Let $\mathbf{x}_i$ denote the normalised feature vector. A sequence of length $T$ is defined as 

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_i = [\mathbf{x}_{i-T+1}, \mathbf{x}_{i-T+2}, \dots, \mathbf{x}_i]
\end{equation}

where:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $T$ is the window size
    \item $\mathbf{X}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times d}$
\end{itemize}

This enables the model to capture temporal dependencies.



\subsection{Train-Test Splitting Strategy}

To evaluate the generalisation capability, both intra-dataset and cross-dataset evaluations were performed. All datasets were partitioned using a fixed train–test split ratio of 75:25, where 75\% of the data was used for training and 25\% for testing. To ensure reproducibility and consistency across experiments, a deterministic random seed was used during the splitting process. A fixed seed value of 42 was employed for all dataset partitions.

Furthermore, each dataset was independently processed for binary and multiclass classification tasks. In the binary setting, the labels were mapped to the benign and attack classes, whereas in the multiclass setting, the original attack categories were preserved. The train–test splitting procedure was applied separately for each classification setting to maintain consistency in the evaluation.

This controlled splitting strategy ensured a fair comparison across datasets and prevented data leakage, thereby enhancing the reliability of the experimental results.

\subsubsection{Intra-Dataset Evaluation}

Each dataset was split as follows:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}^{(k)} = \mathcal{D}^{(k)}_{train} \cup \mathcal{D}^{(k)}_{test}
\end{equation}

using a standard 75:25 ratio, respectively.



\subsubsection{Cross-Dataset Evaluation}

To assess robustness, the model was trained on one dataset and tested on the others.

\begin{equation}
f_\theta : \mathcal{D}^{(train)} \rightarrow \mathcal{D}^{(test)}
\end{equation}

This setup evaluated the ability of the model to generalise across different IoT environments.



\subsection{Data Balancing}

Class imbalance was addressed using the following methods:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Random undersampling of majority class
    \item Oversampling using SMOTE (if required)
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Summary of Preprocessing Pipeline}

The complete preprocessing pipeline can be summarised as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Dataset collection
    \item Label unification
    \item Feature harmonization
    \item Missing value imputation
    \item Encoding of categorical features
    \item Feature normalization
    \item Temporal sequence construction
\end{enumerate}

\section{Experimental Setup}

This section describes the implementation details, training configuration, evaluation protocols, and reproducibility settings used to validate the proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM-attention framework.

\subsection{Implementation Environment}

All experiments were performed using Python with a PyTorch deep-learning framework. The system supports both CPU and GPU executions with automatic device selection based on the hardware availability.

Let $\mathcal{M}_\theta$ denote the proposed model with parameters $\theta$. Training and evaluation were performed on

\begin{itemize}
    \item Programming Language: Python 3.x
    \item Deep Learning Framework: PyTorch
    \item Libraries: NumPy, Pandas, Scikit-learn
    \item Explainability Tools: SHAP, LIME
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Datasets and Experimental Configuration}

The experiments were conducted on four heterogeneous IoT datasets.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Apose-IoT-23
    \item BoT-IoT
    \item CIC-IoMT-2024
    \item CIC-IoT-2025
\end{itemize}

Each dataset undergoes preprocessing and harmonisation, as described in SectionX.

Binary classification was primarily considered.

\begin{equation}
y \in \{0,1\}
\end{equation}

where $0$ represents benign traffic and $1$ denotes a DDoS attack.



\subsection{Training Configuration}

The model was trained using the AdamW optimiser. The training objective was to minimise the categorical cross-entropy loss.

The CNN--LSTM fusion model was trained using the AdamW optimiser with an initial learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 128. The model was trained for 20 epochs without early stopping. The cross-entropy loss function was used for both binary and multiclass classification tasks.

The training configuration of the proposed CNN--LSTM fusion model is summarised in Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams}. These hyperparameters were kept consistent across all datasets to ensure a fair comparison and reproducibility.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Training Hyperparameters for CNN--LSTM Fusion Model}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Optimizer & AdamW \\
Learning Rate & 0.001 \\
Batch Size & 128 \\
Epochs & 20 \\
Early Stopping Patience & 0 (Disabled) \\
Loss Function & CrossEntropy \\
Hardware & CUDA-enabled GPU \\
Train-Test Split & 75:25 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}



\begin{equation}
\theta^* = \arg\min_{\theta} \mathcal{L}(\theta)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = - \sum_{i=1}^{N} y_i \log(\hat{y}_i)
\end{equation}

The training hyperparameters were defined as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Number of epochs: 20
    \item Batch size: 128
    \item Learning rate: $1 \times 10^{-3}$
    \item Weight decay: $1 \times 10^{-4}$
    \item Early stopping patience: 0 (Disabled)
\end{itemize}

The model was trained for 20 epochs without any early stopping. The best-performing model checkpoint was selected for evaluation based on the lowest validation loss obtained during training.




\subsection{Multi-Run Evaluation and Reproducibility}

To ensure statistical robustness, each experiment was repeated $R$ times with different random seeds.

\begin{equation}
R = 5
\end{equation}

Let $\theta_r$ denote the model trained with seed $r$, and let the final performance be reported as the average value.

\begin{equation}
\bar{M} = \frac{1}{R} \sum_{r=1}^{R} M_r
\end{equation}

All sources of randomness were controlled using deterministic seed initialisation in NumPy, Python, and PyTorch. Additionally, all dataset splits were generated using a fixed random seed of 42 to ensure consistency across multiple runs and enable the exact reproducibility of the experimental results.



\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

The model performance was evaluated using the following standard classification metrics:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Accuracy
    \item Precision
    \item Recall
    \item F1-score
    \item ROC-AUC
\end{itemize}

The precision, Recall, and F1-score are computed as

\begin{equation}
\text{Precision} = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\text{Recall} = \frac{TP}{TP + FN}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
F1 = \frac{2 \cdot \text{Precision} \cdot \text{Recall}}{\text{Precision} + \text{Recall}}
\end{equation}

Additionally, calibration metrics, such as the Brier score, were computed to assess probabilistic reliability.



\subsection{Cross-Dataset Generalization Evaluation}

To evaluate the robustness, cross-dataset experiments were conducted as follows:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_\theta : \mathcal{D}^{(train)} \rightarrow \mathcal{D}^{(test)}
\end{equation}

The model was trained on one dataset and tested on unseen datasets to measure generalisation performance.



\subsection{Ablation Study Configuration}

Ablation experiments were performed to assess the contributions of the feature subsets and model components.

Let $\mathcal{F}_s \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ denote the selected feature subset. The model is retrained using

\begin{equation}
X_s = X[:, \mathcal{F}_s]
\end{equation}

Performance degradation was analysed with respect to

\begin{itemize}
    \item Number of features
    \item Model accuracy and F1-score
    \item Computational complexity
\end{itemize}

Model complexity was estimated using:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Number of trainable parameters
    \item Estimated multiply-accumulate operations (MACs)
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Explainability Analysis}

The explainability was evaluated using SHAP, LIME and ANOVA.

\begin{itemize}
    \item SHAP: Global feature importance
    \item LIME: Local instance-level explanations
    \item ANOVA: Statistical feature significance
\end{itemize}

Sampling strategies were applied for scalability as follows.

\begin{itemize}
    \item SHAP sample size: 1024
    \item LIME perturbation samples: 32
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Inference and Efficiency Evaluation}

The inference performance was evaluated using runtime and energy metrics.

Let $t_{inf}$ denote the inference time. The energy consumption proxy is defined as follows:

\begin{equation}
E = P \cdot t_{inf}
\end{equation}

where $P$ is the assumed power consumption (in Watts).

The efficiency metrics included the following:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Inference latency (seconds)
    \item Throughput (samples per second)
    \item Energy consumption (Joules)
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Output Artifacts and Logging}

The experimental pipeline generated the following outputs.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Model checkpoints (.pt)
    \item Training history (CSV)
    \item Performance metrics (JSON, CSV)
    \item Visualization plots (EPS format)
    \item XAI feature importance reports
\end{itemize}

These artefacts ensure reproducibility and facilitate detailed analysis.

\section{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results}

This section presents a comprehensive evaluation of the proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM-Attention framework across multiple IoT datasets. The analysis includes training convergence, classification performance, computational complexity, cross-dataset generalisation, ablation studies, and explainability results.

\subsection{Training Convergence Analysis}

The convergence behaviour of the proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM--Attention model was evaluated using training and validation accuracy curves across all datasets under both binary and multiclass configurations. The training histories were monitored for exactly 20 epochs. The training and validation accuracy curves for the binary classification tasks across the four benchmark datasets are illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_accuracy}, while the corresponding curves for the multiclass classification tasks are presented in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_accuracy}.

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_accuracy} and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_accuracy}, the proposed framework exhibits stable optimization paths and rapid convergence rates:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Apose-IoT-23:} For the binary task (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_accuracy}a), the model converges quickly, with both training and validation accuracies exceeding 99\% by the 5th epoch and stabilizing at 99.31\%. In the multiclass task (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_accuracy}a), the model climbs steadily and converges to its best validation accuracy of 98.44\% by Epoch 15. Due to the compact size of this dataset, training is highly efficient.
    \item \textbf{BoT-IoT:} On the massive BoT-IoT dataset, the model exhibits outstanding training efficiency. For binary classification (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_accuracy}b), the validation accuracy immediately approaches 99.99\% in the first few epochs and remains flat. In the multiclass configuration (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_accuracy}b), the model reaches its best validation accuracy of 99.72\% within 7 epochs and maintains stability.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoMT-2024:} For the IoMT-specific dataset, both binary (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_accuracy}c) and multiclass (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_accuracy}c) configurations converge rapidly, achieving validation accuracies of 99.83\% and 99.24\% respectively by Epoch 6. The curves for training and validation align closely, showcasing the generalisation power of the spatial-temporal feature mapping.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoT-2025:} The model exhibits immediate convergence on this modern high-dimensional dataset. For binary classification (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_accuracy}d), a flawless 100.00\% validation accuracy is achieved in the very first epoch and maintained throughout. In the multiclass scenario (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_accuracy}d), accuracy reaches 99.997\% and stabilises by Epoch 14.
\end{itemize}

Across all experiments, the training and validation accuracy curves aligned closely without any significant gaps, demonstrating that the regularisation techniques (such as dropout and batch normalisation) combined with the feature harmonisation strategy effectively prevented overfitting. The rapid convergence behaviour —often within the first 5 to 15 epochs—further highlights the efficiency of the hybrid CNN--BiLSTM representation, where spatial convolutions capture localised header structures and bidirectional LSTMs model sequence dynamics in network flows.

