As observed, binary classification consistently achieves near-perfect performance across all datasets, with an accuracy and F1-score exceeding 99\% and reaching 100.0\% accuracy on the modern CIC-IoT-2025 dataset. In the multiclass classification tasks, which represent a more challenging and realistic threat scenario with multiple classes of network flows, the model continues to perform strongly, achieving 98.44\% accuracy on Apose-IoT-23, 99.72\% on BoT-IoT, 99.24\% on CIC-IoMT-2024, and 99.997\% on CIC-IoT-2025.

To validate the performance of the proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM--Attention model against existing literature, a state-of-the-art (SOTA) comparison is presented in Table~\ref{tab:sota_comparison}. Across all four benchmark datasets, the proposed framework demonstrated superior or highly competitive performance. On Apose-IoT-23, our model outperformed \cite{pecorella2021iot} by a significant margin and slightly improved upon the DDoSHield proposed by \cite{hnamte2024iot}, reaching an accuracy of 99.31\% and an F1-score of 99.30\%. On the massive BoT-IoT dataset, the proposed framework achieved near-perfect classification (99.99\% accuracy and F1-score), outperforming the classical ML baseline of \cite{moustafa2015bot} and exceeding the CNN-LSTM model of \cite{yan2023deep} which does not incorporate bidirectional LSTM and attention refinement. For the IoMT-specific CIC-IoMT-2024, our model exceeded the RF baseline from \cite{iomt2024dataset} and the explainable DL framework by \cite{shaaban2023biomt}, proving the effectiveness of the parallel CNN and BiLSTM feature processing. Finally, on the high-dimensional CIC-IoT-2025 dataset, our model achieved 100.00\% binary classification accuracy and 99.997\% multiclass accuracy, surpassing the standalone CNN model of \cite{ciciot2025dataset} and the BiLSTM of \cite{habib2023multi}.

