Table~\ref{tab:detailed_class_performance} compiles the detailed class-by-class performance metrics for all eight evaluation runs, providing a complete breakdown of precision, recall, F1-score, and support. As observed, the proposed framework handles both benign and attack flows with high precision. For binary tasks, benign traffic is correctly identified with F1-scores of 99.20\% (Apose-IoT-23), 90.31\% (BoT-IoT), 95.92\% (CIC-IoMT-2024), and 100.00\% (CIC-IoT-2025), showing that false-alarm rates are extremely low. In multiclass classification, the framework demonstrates high robustness in identifying specific DDoS subclasses. For instance, Okiru and PortScan in Apose-IoT-23 are classified with F1-scores of 100.00\% and 97.44\%, respectively. For BoT-IoT multiclass, dominant attack categories such as TCP and UDP are classified with F1-scores of 99.98\% and 100.00\%. The minor performance degradation observed in rare subclasses (such as Keylogging or Data Exfiltration in BoT-IoT, and Malformed Data or OS Scan in CIC-IoMT-2024) is a result of extreme data scarcity (e.g., only 29 support flows for Data Exfiltration compared to over 9.9 million for UDP). For the modern CIC-IoT-2025 multiclass dataset, the framework achieves near-flawless multiclass separation across all eight classes (including bruteforce, mitm, and web attacks), with F1-scores exceeding 99.96\% for all categories, confirming that the attention-based refinement effectively distinguishes diverse threat signatures.

