To further analyse the model behaviour, the confusion matrices for the proposed framework across all benchmark datasets are illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_confusion_matrix} for binary classification and in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_confusion_matrix} for multiclass classification. Finally, Figure~\ref{fig:calibration_curve} presents the probability calibration curves, validating that the model outputs are well-calibrated and represent reliable probability estimates.

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_confusion_matrix}, the binary confusion matrices demonstrate near-perfect classification diagonals. For Apose-IoT-23 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_confusion_matrix}a), the model shows extremely low false alarm and leakage rates. For the massive BoT-IoT dataset (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_confusion_matrix}b), the model successfully classifies almost all 18.34 million attack flows while maintaining a robust detection of benign traffic. On the CIC-IoMT-2024 dataset (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_confusion_matrix}c), the framework separates benign and malicious flows with high precision. For CIC-IoT-2025 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_confusion_matrix}d), the confusion matrix reveals a flawless diagonal, with zero misclassifications between benign and attack categories.

In the multiclass scenario (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_confusion_matrix}), the proposed model maintains a highly dominant diagonal structure across all datasets, even when faced with high-dimensional class distributions and extreme class imbalance:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Apose-IoT-23 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_confusion_matrix}a):} The model achieves 100\% classification accuracy for Okiru and exhibits strong separation for CandC, DDoS, and benign traffic. Only minor leakage was observed for PortScan instances misclassified as CandC.
    \item \textbf{BoT-IoT (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_confusion_matrix}b):} The high-volume TCP and UDP attack classes, which represent over 99.9\% of the test set, are classified near-perfectly. Some minor categories, such as Keylogging and Data Exfiltration, exhibit misclassification into major classes owing to the extreme shortage of training samples for these rare classes.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoMT-2024 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_confusion_matrix}c):} Despite the complexity of classifying 19 distinct categories, the framework preserves a solid diagonal. High-volume MQTT and TCP/IP DDoS/DoS flows were classified with near perfection, whereas some minor reconnaissance categories (e.g. OS Scan and VulScan) showed minor overlap with benign traffic.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoT-2025 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_confusion_matrix}d):} The framework achieves near-perfect classification across all 8 classes, including web attacks and bruteforce, showing that the model scales exceptionally well to modern, complex traffic profiles.
\end{itemize}

