The results indicate that the proposed model achieves extremely low inference latency (consistently around 0.011 to 0.012 ms per sample) and high throughput (between 78,000 and 87,000 samples per second on a CUDA-enabled GPU). This performance makes the framework highly suitable for real-time intrusion detection. The training time ranges from less than a minute for smaller datasets, such as Apose-IoT-23, to approximately 14 hours for the massive BoT-IoT dataset (which contains over 18 million samples). The energy consumption proxy, computed assuming a typical 120 W power rating, tracks the total processing duration, indicating efficient resource usage per sample processed.

To provide a granular view of the system performance, Table~\ref{tab:inference_complexity_detailed} highlights the detailed inference complexity metrics extracted from \texttt{complexity\_fusion.json}. The measured sample latency was extremely small, ranging from 0.0115 to 0.0127 ms. This allows the model to process up to 87,303 samples per second on a single GPU. The total validation detection latency (\texttt{val\_detect\_total\_sec}) tracks the total validation time, which is proportional to the size of the test split (for instance, it takes 223.79 seconds for the 18.3 million test samples of BoT-IoT). The validation energy consumption proxy, computed based on the hardware execution runtime, demonstrated that the model was highly efficient per sample processed, making it suitable for edge-based network nodes.

