As shown in the table, the model trained on BoT-IoT yielded binary accuracies of 96.4\% on Apose-IoT-23, 95.9\% on CIC-IoMT-2024, and 96.8\% on CIC-IoT-2025. Similarly, the model trained on CIC-IoT-2025 achieved cross-dataset accuracies of 97.4\% on BoT-IoT, 97.1\% on Apose-IoT-23, and 96.8\% on CIC-IoMT-2024. These findings demonstrate that feature harmonisation across different feature representations successfully enables the model to retain a strong predictive performance on unseen IoT traffic patterns, with minimal degradation compared to the intra-dataset performance.

\subsection{Ablation Study}

Ablation experiments were conducted to evaluate the contributions of the parallel branches and model components. The results indicate that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Standalone CNN Branch: Captures local feature correlations but has a slightly lower performance (degradation of 1.2--2.5\% in multiclass F1-score) because it lacks sequential traffic modeling.
    \item Standalone BiLSTM Branch: Captures temporal sequence structure but fails to capture spatial feature correlations, resulting in a degradation of 0.8--1.9\% in F1-score.
    \item Proposed Fusion with Attention: Combines the spatial and temporal feature spaces, while the attention mechanism dynamically weights features. This combination achieves the highest overall accuracy and F1-score across all classification tasks.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Explainability Analysis}

To provide interpretability and validate the model decisions, explainability was evaluated using SHAP, LIME, and ANOVA F-statistic significance rankings. The global feature attributions derived using SHAP are presented in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_shap} for binary classification tasks and in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_shap} for multiclass classification tasks.

As illustrated in the global SHAP summary plots (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_shap} and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_shap}), the feature contributions vary across datasets, reflecting the specific network collection environments and threat patterns:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Apose-IoT-23 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_shap}a and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_shap}a):} The flow timestamp (\texttt{ts}), responder port (\texttt{id.resp\_p}), and network protocol (\texttt{proto}) represent the highest Shapley attributions. High values of \texttt{ts} and specific responder ports (e.g., port 80 or 23) contribute positively to the anomaly probability, which corresponds to volumetric scanning or flooding behaviours over time.
    \item \textbf{BoT-IoT (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_shap}b and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_shap}b):} The traffic direction (\texttt{dir}), total packet count (\texttt{pkts}), source packet count (\texttt{spkts}), and source bytes (\texttt{sbytes}) are the dominant features. A high outbound-to-inbound packet ratio (unidirectional traffic) and high transmission rates are strong positive indicators of DDoS flooding.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoMT-2024 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_shap}c and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_shap}c):} The flow duration, packet rates, and protocol flags (e.g. TCP SYN/RST) are highly relevant. Disruptions in connection periodicity (typical of periodic medical telemetry) and spikes in connection lengths indicate active DDoS connections.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoT-2025 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_shap}d and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_shap}d):} The category label attributes (\texttt{label4}), flow timing attributes (\texttt{timestamp}), maximum segment size (\texttt{network\_mss\_max}), and source IP address density (\texttt{network\_ips\_src}) are the primary features. High density of distinct source IPs and anomalous segment sizes indicate distributed reflection or direct flooding.
\end{itemize}

These SHAP findings are validated by the ANOVA F-statistic scores presented in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_anova} for the binary classification tasks and in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_anova} for the multiclass classification tasks. The F-statistic evaluates whether the variance of features between benign and attack classes is significantly larger than the variance within classes, confirming the statistical significance of the feature distributions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Apose-IoT-23 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_anova}a and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_anova}a):} The temporal flow metrics (such as \texttt{ts}) and target address fields (such as \texttt{id.resp\_p}) achieve extremely high F-statistics ($F \approx 22,791$ for \texttt{ts} and $F \approx 20,276$ for \texttt{id.resp\_p}), validating their high variance and separation capability between malicious scan patterns and benign telemetry.
    \item \textbf{BoT-IoT (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_anova}b and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_anova}b):} The unidirectional direction metric (\texttt{dir}) and volumetric metrics (\texttt{pkts}, \texttt{spkts}) show massive F-statistics (e.g., $F \approx 2.78 \times 10^6$ for \texttt{dir}), demonstrating that traffic direction and transmission density are statistically the most discriminative variables.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoMT-2024 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_anova}c and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_anova}c):} Volumetric features such as flow duration, connection intervals, and packet transmission rates exhibit high F-statistics, proving that DDoS reflection/flooding disrupts the typical low-volume medical telemetry profiles.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoT-2025 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_anova}d and Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_multiclass_anova}d):} The categorical label attributes (\texttt{label4}), flow timing attributes (\texttt{timestamp}), maximum segment size (\texttt{network\_mss\_max}), and source IP address density (\texttt{network\_ips\_src}) achieve high F-scores, confirming their statistical significance in detecting Reflection and Direct DDoS attacks.
\end{itemize}

Local interpretability and flow-level decision boundaries are verified using Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) for representative anomalous flows across all evaluated datasets, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_lime}. LIME approximates the complex hybrid model locally using a sparse linear surrogate model to reveal feature contributions for specific classification events:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Apose-IoT-23 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_lime}a):} For a representative anomalous flow, LIME shows that targeting responder port 80 (\texttt{id.resp\_p} $= 80$) with a very small response byte count (\texttt{resp\_bytes} $\le$ 0.01) contributes positively to the attack prediction with 99.8\% confidence, which is typical for HTTP GET flooding attacks.
    \item \textbf{BoT-IoT (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_lime}b):} For a volumetric attack flow, LIME indicates that when a flow exhibits an outbound traffic direction (\texttt{dir} represents unidirectional communication) and packet rates exceed the standardized mean, the local surrogate model assigns a 100\% probability to the DDoS class, proving how the model identifies intense packet floods.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoMT-2024 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_lime}c):} For a clinical network threat instance, LIME demonstrates that protocol flag anomalies (e.g. active TCP SYN/RST flags) combined with a continuous, long connection duration (disrupting periodic telemetry) correspond to the threat label with near 100\% probability.
    \item \textbf{CIC-IoT-2025 (Figure~\ref{fig:proposed_binary_lime}d):} For a reflective DDoS flow, LIME confirms that anomalous MSS configurations (\texttt{network\_mss\_max} deviation) and high source IP address density (\texttt{network\_ips\_src}) are the primary factors driving the local classification, yielding a 100\% probability for the attack category.
\end{itemize}
This multilevel interpretability confirms that the decision-making process of the proposed hybrid architecture is globally consistent, locally transparent, and statistically validated.

