\subsection{Discussion}

The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM--Attention framework provides high detection accuracy (exceeding 98.4\% on all datasets in all settings), strong generalisation capability across networks, robust performance against false positives, and interpretability through integrated XAI methods. Compared to single-architecture models and traditional machine learning baselines, the proposed hybrid approach offers a superior balance between classification accuracy, interpretability, and real-time inference efficiency.

\subsubsection{Sufficiency of the Multi-Level Explainability Validation}
A major challenge in contemporary intrusion detection systems is the lack of sufficient validation for model decisions. Often, studies rely on a single XAI method (such as only SHAP or only LIME), which exposes the validation to the specific mathematical biases of that method. For instance, LIME explanations can be unstable due to random neighborhood sampling, while SHAP can produce highly dispersed feature importances that lack local, flow-level actionability.

By testing ANOVA, SHAP, and LIME simultaneously, our framework achieves a state of validation sufficiency. The three layers of explanations cross-validate each other:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Statistical vs. Algorithmic Cross-Verification:} Features that achieve high F-statistics in ANOVA (e.g., $F \approx 2.78 \times 10^6$ for \texttt{dir} in BoT-IoT) are independently found to have the highest global attribution in SHAP, and are further verified in LIME as the key drivers of individual classification thresholds. This consensus between model-agnostic statistical tests (ANOVA) and neural network feature attribution (SHAP, LIME) guarantees that the model has captured physical traffic characteristics rather than random correlations or dataset biases.
    \item \textbf{Global-Local Complementarity:} While the global SHAP summaries outline the general feature hierarchies across thousands of test flows, the LIME local explanations show the specific trigger values (e.g., port 80 targeting or TCP flag combinations) that cause a single alert. This ensures that validation is sufficient for both high-level network design and low-level security auditing.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Efficiency of the Explainability Demonstration}
Furthermore, the proposed XAI framework balances validation completeness with computational efficiency, presenting an optimized explainability pipeline for real-world deployments. Explainability tools are often criticised for high computational overhead, which can bottleneck real-time security systems. Our framework demonstrates how explainability can be run efficiently through a tiered invocation strategy:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Computational Offloading:} ANOVA is computationally trivial ($\mathcal{O}(N \cdot d)$) and is performed entirely offline or during preprocessing to establish baseline feature significance, requiring zero model query overhead.
    \item \textbf{Tiered Execution at Runtime:} SHAP computation ($\mathcal{O}(2^d)$) is computationally heavy and is reserved for periodic offline global profiling or model retraining phases to update the security policy. In contrast, LIME ($\mathcal{O}(M \cdot d)$) is computationally lighter and is invoked dynamically only when an alert is generated, explaining specific anomalous incidents on-the-fly without the need to calculate global shapley values for every flow.
\end{itemize}
This combination of low-complexity statistical testing (ANOVA) for baseline filtering, cooperative game theory (SHAP) for periodic global verification, and local perturbation (LIME) for on-the-fly flow diagnosis establishes an optimal trade-off, making the overall verification both mathematically rigorous (sufficient) and computationally viable (efficient) for resource-constrained IoT security gateways.

It is important to note that the experiments in this study were conducted using offline network traffic traces from well-established public IoT/IoMT benchmark datasets rather than deploying the model in a live, real-world physical IoT network environment. This approach is highly deliberate and aligns with standard methodologies in cybersecurity research for several reasons. First, deploying unverified deep learning architectures directly onto live operational networks poses substantial operational risks, including potential network downtime, communication latency, and service disruption to critical IoT nodes (especially medical devices in IoMT settings). Second, public benchmark datasets provide diverse heterogeneous traffic patterns and standardised attack labels that are essential for evaluating the applicability and generalisation of models in a controlled manner. Offline validation of these large-scale traces is a necessary prerequisite for verifying the structural robustness, computational footprint (latency and energy proxies), and interpretability of the hybrid architecture before transitioning to a physical hardware deployment. Thus, the findings of this study establish a reliable and verified foundation for future edge integration without the overhead and risk of direct, live testing.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This paper presented a hybrid deep learning framework for DDoS detection in IoT networks, integrating CNN, BiLSTM, and an attention mechanism. The proposed approach was designed to address key challenges in IoT intrusion detection, including heterogeneous data sources, limited generalisation, lack of temporal modelling, and absence of interpretability.

An experimental evaluation conducted on multiple benchmark datasets, namely, Apose-IoT-23, BoT-IoT, CIC-IoMT-2024, and CIC-IoT-2025, demonstrated that the proposed model achieved high detection performance in both binary and multi-class classification settings. The model consistently exhibited strong convergence behaviour, high accuracy, and minimal overfitting across all datasets. Furthermore, a cross-dataset evaluation confirmed the robustness and generalisation capability of the framework, indicating its suitability for heterogeneous IoT environments in the real world.

The integration of XAI techniques, including SHAP, LIME, and ANOVA, provides valuable insights into model decision-making and enhances transparency and trustworthiness. Ablation studies highlighted the importance of feature subsets and model components, whereas efficiency analyses demonstrated the feasibility of the proposed approach for real-time deployment. The proposed hybrid CNN--BiLSTM-Attention framework offers a comprehensive solution that balances accuracy, interpretability, and computational efficiency for IoT DDoS detection.

Despite these promising results, several directions for future research remain. First, extending the framework to handle zero-day and evolving attack patterns would further enhance its robustness. Second, incorporating lightweight model optimisation techniques, such as model pruning and quantisation, could improve deployments on resource-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) devices. Third, exploring federated or distributed learning approaches may enable privacy-preserving intrusion detection across multiple IoT domains. Finally, integrating real-time streaming data processing and online learning mechanisms could further improve adaptability in dynamic network environments.


